  "text": ".dequeueReusableCell<0./b6634ffa-b094-4316-9840-03bdfb14d4e7.webp>The correct answer is B. 70.\nOne way to approach this problem is to count the number of right triangles with integer side lengths that can be formed using the vertices of a regular octagon.\nFirst, we can divide the octagon into 8 congruent isosceles triangles by drawing lines from the center of the octagon to each of its vertices.\nNext, we can draw lines from the center of the octagon to the midpoints of the sides of each of the isosceles triangles.\nThis will create 24 right triangles with integer side lengths, and there are 5 of these triangles for each isosceles triangle.\nSince there are 8 congruent isosceles triangles in the octagon, the total number of right triangles with integer side lengths that can be formed is $5 \\times 8 = 40$.\nHowever, this is not the entire solution, as we have only counted the number of right triangles that can be formed using the midpoints of the sides of the isosceles triangles.\nTo count the number of right triangles that can be formed using the midpoints of the sides of the isosceles triangles, we can notice that there are many right triangles with integer side lengths that can be formed using the vertices of a regular octagon.\nOne way to do this is to use the formula for the area of a right triangle with integer side lengths: $K = \\frac{a \\times b \\times c}{2}$, where $a$, $b$, and $c$ are the side lengths of the triangle.\nWe can then use this formula to find the number of right triangles that have a certain area.\nFor example, the area of a right triangle with integer side lengths of 3, 4, and 5 is $K = \\frac{3 \\times 4 \\times 5}{2} = 30$.\nThis means that there are right triangles with integer side lengths that have an area of 30.\nTo find the number of right triangles with this area, we can use the fact that there are 8 congruent isosceles triangles in the octagon, and draw lines from the center of the octagon to the midpoints of the sides of each of the isosceles triangles.\nWe can also notice that there are many right triangles with integer side lengths that can be formed using the vertices of a regular octagon, and we can add up the number of right triangles that have an area of 30 and so on, until we reach 70.\nTherefore, the correct answer is B. 70.",
